## DESCRIPTION
## A problem that asks if a set is a basis, find the representation of a vector in the basis
## and the dimension of the vector space.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Linear Algebra')
## DBchapter('Abstract Vector Spaces')
## DBsection('Basis and Dimension')
## Date('10/3/2014')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

# updated 2022.10.30

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",         "PGML.pl",
    "parserCheckboxList.pl", 'parserRadioMultiAnswer.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Vector");
Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

# Define all variables used in the problem statement and solution

$a1 = random(-5, 5);
$a2 = random(-5, 5);

$ans1b = Vector($a1, $a2);

$rma1 = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['\(B\) is not a basis of \(V\).'],
        [
            '\(B\) is a basis of \(V\) and \(\text{Rep}_B(\vec{v})=\) %s '
                . 'and the dimension of \(V\) is %s',
            $ans1b,
            Real(2)
        ]
    ],
    1
);

$zero = Vector(0, 0, 0);
do {
    $B = Matrix([
        [ random(-3, 3), 0,             random(-3, 3) ],
        [ random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), 0 ],
        [ random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3) ]
    ]);
    } until $B->det != 0
    && $B->row(1) != $zero
    && $B->row(2) != $zero
    && $B->row(3) != $zero
    && Vector($B->column(1)) != $zero
    && Vector($B->column(2)) != $zero
    && Vector($B->column(3)) != $zero;

$ans2b = Vector(
    non_zero_random(-4, 4),
    non_zero_random(-4, 4),
    non_zero_random(-4, 4)
);

$d = $B->transpose * $ans2b;

$poly1 =
    Formula($B->element(1, 1) . '+ '
        . $B->element(1, 2) . 'x + '
        . $B->element(1, 3)
        . 'x^2')->reduce;
$poly2 =
    Formula($B->element(2, 1) . '+ '
        . $B->element(2, 2) . 'x + '
        . $B->element(2, 3)
        . 'x^2')->reduce;
$poly3 =
    Formula($B->element(3, 1) . '+ '
        . $B->element(3, 2) . 'x + '
        . $B->element(3, 3)
        . 'x^2')->reduce;
$poly4 =
    Formula(($d->value)[0] . '+ '
        . ($d->value)[1] . 'x + '
        . ($d->value)[2]
        . 'x^2')->reduce;

$rma2 = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['\(B\) is not a basis of \(V\).'],
        [
            '\(B\) is a basis of \(V\) and \(\text{Rep}_B(\vec{v})=\) %s '
                . 'and the dimension of \(V\) is %s',
            $ans2b,
            Real(3)
        ]
    ],
    1
);

$rma3 = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['\(B\) is not a basis of \(V\).'],
        [
            '\(B\) is a basis of \(V\) and \(\text{Rep}_B(\vec{v})=\) %s '
                . 'and the dimension of \(V\) is %s',
            Vector(0),
            Real(0)
        ]
    ],
    0
);

$g1 = ColumnVector(random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3));
$g2 = ColumnVector(random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3));
$g3 = non_zero_random(-2, 2) * $g1 + non_zero_random(-2, 2) * $g2;

do {
    $h = ColumnVector(random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4));
} until Vector($h) != $zero;

$p1 = ColumnVector(random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4));
$p2 = ColumnVector(random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4));

$q1    = non_zero_random(-2, 2);
$q2    = non_zero_random(-2, 2);
$p3    = $q1 * $p1 + $q2 * $p2;
$ans4b = Formula("<$q1,$q2>");

$rma4 = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['\(B\) is not a basis of \(V\).'],
        [
            '\(B\) is a basis of \(V\) and \(\text{Rep}_B(\vec{v})=\) %s '
                . 'and the dimension of \(V\) is %s',
            $ans4b,
            Real(2)
        ]
    ],
    1
);

BEGIN_PGML
Each of the following lists a vector space [`V`], a sequence of vectors,
called [` B `]  and a specific vector, [`\vec{v}`].  Determine if the sequence is 
a basis and if the sequence is a basis, find [` \text{Rep}_B(\vec{v}) `]
and the dimension of [`V`].

a)  [``
    V = \mathbb{R}^2, \qquad B = \left\langle 
    \left[\begin{array}{r} 1 \\ 0 \end{array} \right],
    \left[\begin{array}{r} 0 \\ 1 \end{array} \right] \right\rangle, 
    \qquad \qquad
    \vec{v} = \left[\begin{array}{r} [$a1] \\ [$a2] \end{array} \right]
    ``]

    [_]{$rma1}


b) [``
    V={\cal P}_2 \qquad B = \langle [$poly1], [$poly2], [$poly3] \rangle, \qquad \qquad
    \vec{v} = [$poly4]
    ``]

    [_]{$rma2}


c) [``
    V = \mathbb{R}^3 \qquad B = \left\langle 
    [$g1], [$g2], [$g3]
    \right\rangle,
    \qquad \qquad
    \vec{v} = [$h]
    ``]

    [_]{$rma3}


d) [``
    V = \left\{ [$p1] t +  [$p2] s \; | \; s,t \in \mathbb{R} \right\} \qquad
    B = \left\langle [$p1], [$p2] \right\rangle
    \qquad \vec{v} =  [$p3]
    ``]

    [_]{$rma4}

END_PGML

# matrix of part c
$A3 = Matrix([
    [ $g1->{data}[0], $g2->{data}[0], $g3->{data}[0] ],
    [ $g1->{data}[1], $g2->{data}[1], $g3->{data}[1] ],
    [ $g1->{data}[2], $g2->{data}[2], $g3->{data}[2] ]
]);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) To determine if [`B`] is a basis of [`V`], first note that the vectors in [`B`] are linearly independent since they are not multiples of each other.

    To determine if they span [`V`], we solve
    [``` c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + c_2 \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix} ```]
    where the right hand side is a general vector in [`\mathbb{R}^2`]. In this case [`c_1 = x_1, c_2 = x_2`], so there is a solution so it spans the space. 

    To find the representation, since we have the relation [`\vec{c} = \vec{x}`] then the representation is the same as the vector [`\vec{v}`].  The dimension is the number of basis vectors or 2. 

b) To determine if [`B`] is a basis of [`V`], first check if the vectors are linearly independent.  This is found with
    [``` c_1 ([$poly1]) + c_2([$poly2]) + c_3([$poly3]) = 0 ```]
    which is the same as solving the linear system [`A \vec{u} = \vec{0}`] where
    [``` A = [$B] ```]
    and [`\vec{u} = [1 \; x \; x^2 ]^{\intercal}`]. This has a unique solution if the determinant of [`A`] is nonzero and since [`|A| = [@$B->det@] `], the vectors of [`B`] are linearly independent. 
    
    To determine if the vectors span [`{\cal P}_2`], we solve:
    [``` c_1 ([$poly1]) + c_2([$poly2]) + c_3([$poly3]) = w_0 + w_1 x + w_2 x^2 ```], which is the same as solving the linear system [`A\vec{y} = \vec{w}`] where [`\vec{w} = [w_0 \; w_1 \; w_2]^{\intercal} `] and this has a unique solution because the determinant of [`A`] is nonzero.  Thus, [`B`] spans [`{\cal P}_2`]. 
    
    To find the representation we solve 
    [``` c_1 ([$poly1]) + c_2([$poly2]) + c_3([$poly3]) = [$poly4] ```]
    which can be solved via the linear system [`A\vec{u} = \vec{v}`], where [`v`] is the coefficients of the polynomial on the right hand side.  This has the solution that 
    [` \vec{u} = [$ans2b]`], therefore this is the representation.  Also, since there are 3 vectors in [`B`], the dimension is 3. 
    
c) a) To determine if [`B`] is a basis of [`V`], we first check if they are linearly indepependent.  That is, solve
    [``` c_1     [$g1] + c_2 [$g2] + c_3 [$g3] = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} ```]
    
    This will have a unique solution of [`\vec{c} = \vec{0}`] if the determinant of the matrix
    [``` A = [$A3] ```]
    which is 0.  Since [`A`] is singular, the solution to [`A \vec{c} = \vec{0}`] is not unique, so the column are not linearly independent.  Therefore [`B`] is not a basis.
    
d) In this case, first check if [`B`] is a basis.  From inspection, the columns are not multiples of
one another, so they are linearly independent.  Also, since the two vectors in [`B`] are the same vectors
as in the definition of [`V`], so they span the set.  Therefore it is a basis and with two vectors, 
the dimension is 2. 

To find the representation of the vector [`v`] in the basis, we solve 
[``` c_1 [$p1] + c_2 [$p2] = [$p3] ```]

The solution of this is [`c_1= [$q1], c_2 = [$q2]`], so the vector representation is [`[$ans4b]`]. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
